def manuscript():
    old=(P/'archive/before_round2_20261015/TRA-20261014-V3.tex').read_text(encoding='utf-8');head=old[:old.index(r'\clearpage'+'\n'+r'\section{Case study}')]
    abstract=r'''Income polarization and cheaper electrified road travel change both railway demand and the value of regulating service. We formulate a railway design problem linking product coverage, integer departures and fares to elastic participation and nested mode, product and seat choice. Expected income changes class-specific money sensitivity and value of time, while road electrification affects competing travel costs. Profit and railway-centered welfare objectives operate under financial, capacity, service and vulnerable-mobility requirements. Three calibrated Chinese corridors illustrate how instrument choice depends on spare capacity, seat concentration and income composition. Equal-mean uniform and polarized shocks are compared under a common same-state protection convention, separating design effects from macroeconomic participation losses. Fare and frequency contributions are evaluated on a 2.5-percent menu; same-coverage conventional additions enter as frequencies of their parent product. Strict reference-mobility guarantees reveal the cost of discrete operating responses to small trip shortfalls. Period enumeration with fleet coupling certifies reference policies in the two single-OD corridors. The application identifies conditional policy mechanisms whose magnitudes depend on capacity headroom and corridor cost allocation.'''
    head=re.sub(r'\\begin\{abstract\}.*?\\end\{abstract\}',lambda _:r'\begin{abstract}'+'\n'+abstract+'\n'+r'\end{abstract}',head,flags=re.S)
    intro='The application compares railway instruments in three corridors under partial operating authority. Equal-mean income shocks reveal how class composition changes fares, frequencies and seat-pricing value when mobility is protected relative to each current market state. A separate absolute-reference guarantee measures the cost of recovering macroeconomic as well as design-induced losses. Factorial attribution, pooled conventional-service additions and exact single-OD benchmarks connect the policy mechanisms to seat capacity, integer service choices and resource limits. Capacity headroom and network cost exposure define the range of application.'
